--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -6,6 +6,10 @@
 
 The top-level tools are shaped for agents, after [gogcli](https://github.com/openclaw/gogcli)'s typed tool set: `search` (Gmail search syntax, returning thread summaries with sender, subject, date and snippet), `readThread`, `readMessage` and `attachment` (decoded text instead of MIME), `labels` (with unread counts), `send` and `draft` (plain fields; replies keep the thread, subject and references, with reply-all), and `modify` (archive, read, star, trash and labels by name). Reads span every linked account at once unless `account` names one; writes need `account` only when several accounts are linked, and ask for approval. Results name the account they came from by its address, read from the Gmail profile and cached for a day. Their code is `tools/`, copied from `shared/google-tools` by `node scripts/google-vendor.ts`, which also gives the same tools to the all-in-one `google` package. The generated tools described below are under `api`, each taking an `accountId`.
 
+`send` and `draft` take `attachments`, each from one source: `contentBase64`, an https `url` the file downloads from without signing in (nothing is sent to it but the request), or `messageId` with `attachmentId` to forward a file from another message. Together they may total 25 MB, Gmail's limit. The message is built as multipart/mixed and sent as JSON `raw` up to 5 MB; above that it goes by Gmail's resumable upload (`uploadType=resumable`), which also keeps a reply's `threadId`.
+
+Gmail's API cannot schedule a send; that is a Gmail web feature. `send` with `sendAt` (RFC 3339, or a local time with `timeZone`) instead saves the message as a draft and starts the `sendLater` workflow, which sleeps until the time and sends the draft with `drafts.send` from the account that scheduled it. The draft stays in Gmail's Drafts until then, and edits made there go out too. `scheduleDraft` schedules an existing draft or moves a scheduled one, `scheduled` lists them with their state, and `cancelScheduled` stops one and keeps the draft. A draft sent or deleted by hand ends its run quietly. If Gmail refuses the send, the workflow asks the person in Human in the loop whether to send it again or keep the draft. Scheduled sends are recorded in the app's database (`migrations/`) so they can be listed; `scheduled` shows a caller only the sends of their own linked accounts.
+
 ## Interface
 
 OpenAPI, vendored as `gmail-openapi.json`. Google publishes the Gmail API only as a Discovery document, so `discovery-to-openapi.mjs` (derived from `google-drive`'s converter, with Gmail's settings) converts Google's own document (`https://gmail.googleapis.com/$discovery/rest?version=v1`, **revision 20261109**) into OpenAPI 3. The output is checked in so any upstream change shows up in review. To refresh: `curl -s 'https://gmail.googleapis.com/$discovery/rest?version=v1' -o /tmp/g.json && node packages/gmail/discovery-to-openapi.mjs /tmp/g.json > packages/gmail/gmail-openapi.json`, update the revision here and in `openapi.ts`, then run `node scripts/google-vendor.ts`. The output is sorted, because Google returns the document's keys in a different order on each fetch. Every operation keeps Google's standard query parameters (`fields`, `quotaUser`, `prettyPrint` and the rest) except the credential ones (`access_token`, `oauth_token`, `key`): the account supplies credentials, and a value given as a tool argument would land in the request URL and the approval prompt. Request bodies are optional, as before, except for the six methods that take a whole message. Every operation is tagged with its Discovery resource, so the tools stay under `users` as before, and carries a plain bearer requirement.
